may constrain dispatcher-owned workers while delegated children can exist outside those independent counters.

The design must therefore reason about a HOST-WIDE resource budget, not just another per-board or per-profile semaphore.

## Deadlock must be explicitly solved

A naive budget can deadlock:

1. parent acquires the last local-worker slot;
2. parent delegates a child;
3. child needs a slot;
4. parent waits for child;
5. child waits for parent to release slot.

The proposed design/tests must demonstrate the intended policy for this case.

For example, the design may use one of these patterns or another explicitly justified mechanism:

- delegation transfers/re-lends parent capacity;
- parent and its delegated tree share one accounting lease;
- child reservation is made atomically before parent waits;
- parent releases a resource-class lease while awaiting child;
- separate execution-count and model-residency budgets.

Do NOT simply increase the host limit to hide the deadlock.

The test suite must prove that the chosen policy cannot oversubscribe local model residency and cannot deadlock the common parent-awaits-child path.

## Provider-aware accounting

Coordinate with the runtime/concurrency owner.

Current operator policy intentionally allows cloud workers overnight. Therefore the host-wide budget should not blindly count every worker process as equivalent local GPU pressure.

The design must state what resource is actually being bounded.

At minimum distinguish:

`local_model_backed`

from:

`cloud_model_backed`

and any relevant:

`no_model / utility`

worker class.

A cloud worker should not consume a local model-residency slot merely because it is a Hermes worker.

Conversely, delegated local children must not evade the host-wide local budget merely because their parent was admitted through another profile/board.

Do not overwrite or race the separate provider-aware concurrency work. Reconcile ownership/source boundaries before proposing code integration.

